{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793574037,"nonce":"ujZZBt1jl98Jp8IhFLVkuEBnPsA=","title":"scalar_replace_alloc depends on how many blocks have merged: Lua operator overloading loses its elimination","message":"Lua op_overload (`acc = acc + a` with `__add` returning a fresh table, ten million iterations twice) runs in 32 ms because round 2 of the optimizer scalar-replaces the temporary tables. With `cfg_simplify::prune_unreachable_module` called at the start of every round (7af5d4ba, removed in 1cab2a1b), round 1 merges 3 more blocks, round 2 reports `sra 0` instead of `sra 2`, five mallocs remain in the tier-1 body instead of three and the benchmark takes 97 ms. The pass accepts an allocation only when the malloc and every use of its pointer, including terminators and phi incomings of other blocks, are in one block; the extra merges leave a use in another block, so the candidate is rejected (rejections at the terminator check and the cross-block check; ZYNTAX_SRA_DUMP=1 prints the counts).\n\nFix direction: scalar replacement across blocks (the allocation's fields as phis), or forwarding through the returned-table phi of an inlined callee, so the result does not depend on where cfg_simplify stops merging. Repro: re-add the prune call at the top of the round loop in `run_interp_safe_opts_with` and run `benchmarks/op_overload.lua` with ZYLUA_LLVM=1.","files":null}]}